[{"record_id":"full_2026-07-06__rec_001668","candidate_id":"full_2026-07-06__rec_001668","source_record_id":"rec_001668","source_corpus":"uncertain","title":"Decoding Alzheimer's Disease One Cell Class at a Time.","abstract":"Multimodal imaging-based on single-cell genomics and spatial transcriptomics has shed new light on the taxonomy of genetically defined cell clusters in the mammalian brain. While transcriptomic approaches have revolutionized our ability to classify brain cells, their true value emerges when they are interpreted in conjunction with anatomical, physiological, and translational frameworks. Accordingly, significant progress has been made to elucidate relationships between gene expression, electrical and morphological properties of some of these clusters. This rapidly growing body of work shows not only that the cell cluster composition varies across brain regions but also evolves over time and changes during the progression of disease states like Alzheimer's disease. Given this complexity, integrating transcriptomic, structural, and functional data is now becoming essential for drawing meaningful comparisons across studies. In this review, we summarize these findings and discuss how this knowledge base is shifting towards more integrative approaches, quickly chal -lenging current ideas regarding the genetic, molecular, and cellular underpinnings of Alzheimer's disease.\n\nAnnalisa Scimemi ascimemi@albany.edu\n\n1 Department of Laboratory Medicine and Pathology, School of Medicine, University of Washington, Seattle, WA 98104, USA\n\n2 Geriatric Research Education and Clinical Center, VA Puget Sound Health Care System, Seattle, WA 98108, USA\n\n3 Department of Medicine, University of Washington, Seattle, WA 98195, USA [truncated]","doi":"10.1007/s10571-026-01685-y","year":"","venue":"","sources":[],"full_text_context":"[abstract: Abstract]\nMultimodal imaging-based on single-cell genomics and spatial transcriptomics has shed new light on the taxonomy of genetically defined cell clusters in the mammalian brain. While transcriptomic approaches have revolutionized our ability to classify brain cells, their true value emerges when they are interpreted in conjunction with anatomical, physiological, and translational frameworks. Accordingly, significant progress has been made to elucidate relationships between gene expression, electrical and morphological properties of some of these clusters. This rapidly growing body of work shows not only that the cell cluster composition varies across brain regions but also evolves over time and changes during the progression of disease states like Alzheimer's disease. Given this complexity, integrating transcriptomic, structural, and functional data is now becoming essential for drawing meaningful comparisons across studies. In this review, we summarize these findings and discuss how this knowledge base is shifting towards more integrative approaches, quickly chal -lenging current ideas regarding the genetic, molecular, and cellular underpinnings of Alzheimer's disease.\n\nAnnalisa Scimemi ascimemi@albany.edu\n\n1 Department of Laboratory Medicine and Pathology, School of Medicine, University of Washington, Seattle, WA 98104, USA\n\n2 Geriatric Research Education and Clinical Center, VA Puget Sound Health Care System, Seattle, WA 98108, USA\n\n3 Department of Medicine, University of Washington, Seattle, WA 98195, USA [truncated]\n\n[introduction: Introduction]\nTranscriptomics began in the mid-1990s with the development of DNA microarrays and evolved significantly in the late 2000s with the advent of RNA sequencing (RNAseq). The field advanced even further in the 2010s with the emergency of single-cell RNA-seq (scRNA-seq) and spatial transcriptomics. Over the past five years, these technologies have generated an increasingly granular view of the brain, highlighting a level of cellular diversity that was difficult to envision just a few years ago (Maynard et al. 2021; Yao et al. 2023, 2021 ). These high-resolution datasets have deepened our understanding of brain organization under physiological conditions and opened new avenues for exploring disease mechanisms, including those underlying Alzheimer's dis -ease (AD) (Gabitto et al. 2024; Grubman et al. 2019; Liang et al. 2025; Mathys et al. 2019 ). In particular, transcriptomic profiling has been instrumental to characterize the brain cel -lular composition in animal models of AD and in patients affected by this disease, offering insights into how neuro -degeneration alters brain architecture (Gabitto et al. 2024; Grubman et al. 2019; Liang et al. 2025; Mathys et al. 2019 ).\n\n<!-- image -->\n\nDespite these advances, transcriptomic approaches also raise new challenges and limitations (Ecker et al. 2017; Scala et al. 2021; Tasic et al. 2018 ). A fundamental issue is that genetic profiles alone may not fully capture cell identity, because gene expression patterns, and the composition of cell clusters defined by these patterns, are not fixed (Ecker et al. 2017; [truncated]\n\n[discussion_conclusion: Conclusions]\nThe recent advances in transcriptomic profiling underscore the growing need for integrated, multimodal approaches to understand the cellular basis of AD. While different tran -scriptomics approaches have revolutionized our ability to classify brain cells, their true value emerges when they are interpreted together with anatomical, physiological, and translational frameworks. As the field progresses, a sus -tained focus on analytical rigor, cross-species validation, context-aware interpretation, and integration of different datasets will be essential to avoid generating sparse information and bridge the gap between molecular profiles and disease mechanisms, ultimately enabling the development of better models and treatments for AD. A key challenge will be determining whether the molecular changes detected through multimodal analyses are drivers or consequences of AD pathology, and identifying molecular features that confer resistance to AD. Therefore, advancing from descriptive to correlative and causal studies has become imperative to ensure that findings are biologically meaningful, transla -tionally relevant, and useful for identifying biomarkers and refining animal models. With these considerations in place, we believe the future is cautiously bright, with the potential to yield transformative insights into AD and open promising new avenues for its treatment. Perhaps acquiring mul -timodal 'big data' will become easier and more affordable, but making sense of it will continue to be priceless.\n\n<!-- image -->\n\nAuthor Contributions A.S. [truncated]","section_evidence":[{"section_type":"abstract","heading":"Abstract","heading_level":3,"line_start":15,"line_end":40,"text":"Multimodal imaging-based on single-cell genomics and spatial transcriptomics has shed new light on the taxonomy of genetically defined cell clusters in the mammalian brain. While transcriptomic approaches have revolutionized our ability to classify brain cells, their true value emerges when they are interpreted in conjunction with anatomical, physiological, and translational frameworks. Accordingly, significant progress has been made to elucidate relationships between gene expression, electrical and morphological properties of some of these clusters. This rapidly growing body of work shows not only that the cell cluster composition varies across brain regions but also evolves over time and changes during the progression of disease states like Alzheimer's disease. Given this complexity, integrating transcriptomic, structural, and functional data is now becoming essential for drawing meaningful comparisons across studies. In this review, we summarize these findings and discuss how this knowledge base is shifting towards more integrative approaches, quickly chal -lenging current ideas regarding the genetic, molecular, and cellular underpinnings of Alzheimer's disease.\n\nAnnalisa Scimemi ascimemi@albany.edu\n\n1 Department of Laboratory Medicine and Pathology, School of Medicine, University of Washington, Seattle, WA 98104, USA\n\n2 Geriatric Research Education and Clinical Center, VA Puget Sound Health Care System, Seattle, WA 98108, USA\n\n3 Department of Medicine, University of Washington, Seattle, WA 98195, USA [truncated]","original_chars":2335},{"section_type":"introduction","heading":"Introduction","heading_level":3,"line_start":41,"line_end":52,"text":"Transcriptomics began in the mid-1990s with the development of DNA microarrays and evolved significantly in the late 2000s with the advent of RNA sequencing (RNAseq). The field advanced even further in the 2010s with the emergency of single-cell RNA-seq (scRNA-seq) and spatial transcriptomics. Over the past five years, these technologies have generated an increasingly granular view of the brain, highlighting a level of cellular diversity that was difficult to envision just a few years ago (Maynard et al. 2021; Yao et al. 2023, 2021 ). These high-resolution datasets have deepened our understanding of brain organization under physiological conditions and opened new avenues for exploring disease mechanisms, including those underlying Alzheimer's dis -ease (AD) (Gabitto et al. 2024; Grubman et al. 2019; Liang et al. 2025; Mathys et al. 2019 ). In particular, transcriptomic profiling has been instrumental to characterize the brain cel -lular composition in animal models of AD and in patients affected by this disease, offering insights into how neuro -degeneration alters brain architecture (Gabitto et al. 2024; Grubman et al. 2019; Liang et al. 2025; Mathys et al. 2019 ).\n\n<!-- image -->\n\nDespite these advances, transcriptomic approaches also raise new challenges and limitations (Ecker et al. 2017; Scala et al. 2021; Tasic et al. 2018 ). A fundamental issue is that genetic profiles alone may not fully capture cell identity, because gene expression patterns, and the composition of cell clusters defined by these patterns, are not fixed (Ecker et al. 2017; [truncated]","original_chars":4370},{"section_type":"discussion_conclusion","heading":"Conclusions","heading_level":3,"line_start":285,"line_end":302,"text":"The recent advances in transcriptomic profiling underscore the growing need for integrated, multimodal approaches to understand the cellular basis of AD. While different tran -scriptomics approaches have revolutionized our ability to classify brain cells, their true value emerges when they are interpreted together with anatomical, physiological, and translational frameworks. As the field progresses, a sus -tained focus on analytical rigor, cross-species validation, context-aware interpretation, and integration of different datasets will be essential to avoid generating sparse information and bridge the gap between molecular profiles and disease mechanisms, ultimately enabling the development of better models and treatments for AD. A key challenge will be determining whether the molecular changes detected through multimodal analyses are drivers or consequences of AD pathology, and identifying molecular features that confer resistance to AD. Therefore, advancing from descriptive to correlative and causal studies has become imperative to ensure that findings are biologically meaningful, transla -tionally relevant, and useful for identifying biomarkers and refining animal models. With these considerations in place, we believe the future is cautiously bright, with the potential to yield transformative insights into AD and open promising new avenues for its treatment. Perhaps acquiring mul -timodal 'big data' will become easier and more affordable, but making sense of it will continue to be priceless.\n\n<!-- image -->\n\nAuthor Contributions A.S. [truncated]","original_chars":3067}],"docling_markdown":"data/docling_uncertain_no_vlm_2026-07-08/markdown/full_2026_07_06_rec_001668.md"}]

